1
00:00:01,503 --> 00:00:08,503
A fix outlives its reason. You, a script or an agent change something, and a year later nobody knows why it is there.

2
00:00:08,513 --> 00:00:15,513
podshl-repairs keeps a record of such changes, and looks at them again after every update. This is a clean Omarchy.

3
00:00:15,524 --> 00:00:26,600
One program from the release, checked against its sums.

4
00:00:26,604 --> 00:00:32,146
Into your own ~/.local/bin. No root, and no package.

5
00:00:32,150 --> 00:00:35,150
Nothing recorded yet.

6
00:00:35,161 --> 00:00:37,933
A review after every Omarchy update.

7
00:00:39,443 --> 00:00:42,220
And the agent Omarchy is set to use — Claude here — records what it writes, through its own hooks.

8
00:00:42,224 --> 00:00:48,224
Even this change to Claude's settings is in the record, with a copy to go back to.

9
00:00:51,513 --> 00:01:02,118
Ask the agent for a change, the way anybody does.

10
00:01:02,122 --> 00:01:04,896
Nobody typed anything for the record.

11
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:10,900
It is there anyway: by claude, with a copy from before the change.

12
00:01:10,911 --> 00:01:13,688
You do not like it. Undo.

13
00:01:13,693 --> 00:01:17,693
The file is back, byte for byte.

14
00:01:18,214 --> 00:01:29,472
A change you keep: dim the windows you are not using.

15
00:01:29,476 --> 00:01:32,253
Weeks later Omarchy puts its own default back: an update, a migration, a refresh.

16
00:01:32,257 --> 00:01:38,257
Your change is gone, and your rounded corners with it. Nothing tells you.

17
00:01:38,779 --> 00:01:41,558
After every update, Omarchy runs the hook.

18
00:01:41,562 --> 00:01:47,562
This time something does. The notice stays until you look at it.

19
00:01:50,628 --> 00:01:57,628
One click: which change, and why it wants a look.

20
00:01:59,150 --> 00:02:01,926
Undo goes back to before Claude touched the file, so your rounded corners return. Ask again for the dimming, and it is recorded again.

21
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:16,888
Now PODSHL's own client, built from its PKGBUILD.

22
00:02:16,892 --> 00:02:19,992
The AUR step of every omarchy-update:

23
00:02:19,996 --> 00:02:27,996
It asks the AUR about podshl-bin. It is not there, and the name is free: whoever registers it, the next update installs theirs.

24
00:02:28,006 --> 00:02:35,006
The fix is a local repository, a workaround until the package is in the AUR. So it goes in the record, with its reason.

25
00:02:50,302 --> 00:02:53,076
The same step again:

26
00:02:53,080 --> 00:02:58,080
Nothing foreign left, so nothing is looked up.

27
00:02:58,602 --> 00:03:04,387
An older workaround, for a Hyprland bug.

28
00:03:04,391 --> 00:03:13,391
Watched, it asks GitHub, and only because you said so. The issue is closed: time to see whether the workaround can go. It removes nothing by itself.

29
00:03:13,912 --> 00:03:29,720
Code in a repository has a history of its own.

30
00:03:29,724 --> 00:03:36,724
Changed, and not recorded: git already has it. The record is for what has no history.

31
00:03:42,785 --> 00:03:49,785
What was changed on this machine, by whom, and why, looked at again after every update.

32
00:03:49,795 --> 00:03:54,795
podshl-repairs · github.com/dx111ge/podshl · docs/REPAIRS.md

